# Whatsmeow Quickstart

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:whatsmeow_ex, "~> 0.1.0"},
  {:kino, "~> 0.13"}
])
```

## 1. Crypto primitives — RFC-locked

The `Whatsmeow.Crypto.*` module set is a thin wrapper over Erlang's stdlib `:crypto`. All AEAD ciphers, HKDF, and Curve25519 are covered by the published RFC test vectors.

```elixir
alias Whatsmeow.Crypto.{Curve25519, HKDF, AES.GCM}

# X25519 — generate a keypair, agree on a shared secret.
{a_pub, a_priv} = Curve25519.generate_keypair()
{b_pub, b_priv} = Curve25519.generate_keypair()

shared_alice = Curve25519.agree(a_priv, b_pub)
shared_bob = Curve25519.agree(b_priv, a_pub)
shared_alice == shared_bob
```

```elixir
# HKDF-SHA256 — derive 64 bytes.
HKDF.derive("ikm", "salt", "info", 64)
|> Base.encode16(case: :lower)
```

## 2. Binary XML codec

WhatsApp's wire protocol uses a custom binary XML codec. The encoder/decoder is fully reversible.

```elixir
alias Whatsmeow.Binary
alias Whatsmeow.Binary.Node

node = %Node{
  tag: "iq",
  attrs: %{"type" => "set", "to" => "s.whatsapp.net"},
  content: [%Node{tag: "ping", attrs: %{}, content: nil}]
}

encoded = Binary.encode(node)
{:ok, decoded} = Binary.decode(encoded)
decoded == node
```

## 3. Argo — self-describing wire format

```elixir
alias Whatsmeow.Argo

payload = %{
  "users" => [
    %{"id" => 1, "name" => "alice"},
    %{"id" => 2, "name" => "bob"}
  ],
  "count" => 2
}

encoded = Argo.encode(payload)
{:ok, decoded, ""} = Argo.decode(encoded)
decoded
```

## 4. AppState — LTHash

```elixir
alias Whatsmeow.AppState.LTHash

base = <<0::1024>>

# Apply two mutations…
items = ["mut-1", "mut-2"]
h1 = LTHash.add(base, items)

# Removing the same items returns to baseline (commutative).
h2 = LTHash.sub(h1, items)
h2 == base
```

## 5. Signal Double Ratchet — 1:1 messaging

```elixir
alias Whatsmeow.Crypto.Curve25519
alias Whatsmeow.Signal.{Session, SessionCipher}

rk = :crypto.strong_rand_bytes(32)
bob_ratchet = Curve25519.generate_keypair()
{bob_pub, _} = bob_ratchet

alice = Session.init_initiator(rk, bob_pub)
bob = Session.init_responder(rk, bob_ratchet)

{ct, _alice} = SessionCipher.encrypt(alice, "hello bob")
SessionCipher.decrypt(bob, ct)
```

## 6. Subscribing to session events

In a paired application, events fan out via `Phoenix.PubSub`:

```elixir
:ok = Whatsmeow.Notifications.subscribe("my-device-id")

receive do
  {:whatsmeow, %Whatsmeow.Types.Events.QR{code: code}} -> IO.puts(code)
  {:whatsmeow, %Whatsmeow.Types.Events.Connected{}} -> IO.puts("connected!")
end
```

